COPY public.articles (id, slug, title, subtitle, excerpt, content, cover_image, author, author_role, category, tags, read_time, published_at, created_at, updated_at, is_published) FROM stdin;
1f58b730-67a8-440d-b4b9-38b4a7d65f01	anekantavada-part-1-seeing-only-one-side-of-reality	Anekantavada (Part 1): What If We Are Seeing Only One Side of Reality?	Have you ever stopped and asked yourself: Am I seeing the complete reality — or only one side of it?	We often mistake our limited perspective for the entire reality. Explore the profound Jain philosophical doctrine of Anekantavada through the timeless parable of the elephant and discover how asking one simple question can transform relationships and conflicts.	### Have You Ever Been Completely Sure That You Were Right?\n\nYour parents don't understand you.  \nYour colleague is careless.  \nYour friend is ignoring you.  \nYour partner doesn't care.  \nYour boss is unreasonable.  \n\nAnd you're sure about it.\n\nBut have you ever stopped and asked yourself:\n\n> *"Am I seeing the complete reality — or only one side of it?"*\n\nThis simple question takes us to one of the most profound ideas in Jain philosophy: **Anekantavada**.\n\n---\n\n### What Is Anekantavada?\n\n**Anekantavada** is a fundamental doctrine of Jain philosophy that recognizes the many-sided nature of reality.\n\nThe word is commonly understood through the ideas of:\n- **Aneka** — many / multifaceted\n- **Anta** — aspects or sides\n\nIt is therefore described as the doctrine of **many-sidedness** or **non-absolutism**.\n\n![Decoding Anekantavada: Many-Sided Wisdom](/assets/articles/anekantavada-principles.png)\n\nIn simple language:\n> **Anekantavada means that reality has many aspects, while our individual understanding usually sees only some of them.**\n\nThis does not mean that there is no truth. It means that our limited perspective should not automatically be mistaken for the whole truth. And that distinction is at the heart of Anekantavada.\n\n---\n\n### The Elephant: A Simple Way to Understand Anekantavada\n\nThe famous ancient parable of the blind men and the elephant is often used to explain the idea of many-sidedness.\n\nSeveral people encounter an elephant:\n- One touches its **trunk** and describes it as being like a *snake*.\n- Another touches its **leg** and describes it as being like a *pillar*.\n- Another touches its **ear** and compares it to a *fan*.\n- Another touches its **body** and describes it as a *wall*.\n- Another touches its **tail** and calls it a *rope*.\n- Another touches its **tusk** and describes it as a *spear*.\n\n![The Parable of the Elephant & Many-Sided Reality](/assets/articles/blind-men-elephant.png)\n\nEach person has experienced something real. The problem begins when one person insists:\n\n> *"My experience is the entire elephant."*\n\nThe issue is not necessarily that their experience is false. The issue is that an experience of a **partial aspect** has been treated as the **complete reality**.\n\nAnd that is precisely where one-sided thinking (*Ekantavada*) and conflict begin.\n\nThe lesson isn't *"Everyone is right without distinction."*  \nThe real lesson is:\n\n> **Before deciding that someone else is completely wrong, ask what part of reality they may be seeing that you aren't.**\n\n---\n\n### The Question Anekantavada Leaves Us With\n\nWe often ask:\n> *"What happened?"*\n\nAnekantavada encourages us to ask one deeper question:\n> *"How am I seeing what happened?"*\n\nThat single question can change the way we understand people, situations, and even ourselves.\n\nBecause perhaps the biggest problem isn't that we have a perspective. The problem begins when we mistake our perspective for the entire reality.\n\n---\n\n### What Comes Next?\n\nUnderstanding that reality can have many sides is the first step.\n\nBut there is a bigger question awaiting:  \n**How does our mind turn a small event into a major conflict?**\n\nThat is where we discover the difference between what actually happened and what we think happened.	/assets/articles/anekantavada-cover.png	Team I Love Jinshasan	ILJ Research & Philosophy Pod	Jain Philosophy & Wisdom	{Anekantavada,Philosophy,Mindset,Jainism,Self-Awareness}	4 min read	2026-10-17 00:00:00+00	2026-10-17 15:42:17.746191+00	2026-10-17 15:42:17.746191+00	t
\.
