61e4a485-039a-4e84-a649-55a9708d02fe	Jainific passport	Everyone who enters gets a passport with their entry money for free or with cost, depends.And that passport will have different stall names according to age.Like if we think that young adults like teens should definitely visit a particular game we add it to the passport. The passport will also contain a map of the area where the event is being held. To help navigating their way through stalls annd know where exactly they wanna gom. It will also contain information about I❤️jinshashan as an organisation, its current projects, how we are built as an organisation and how we are functioning.	134f3fc8-8546-4bb3-b39a-e671bfcb0808	4842d49f-c3ea-47a4-bfee-03837bec04a5	reviewed	Thank you for your idea. We will definitely think on this. Just keep  Suggesting such  wonderful ideas	2026-04-03 04:08:32.10104+00	2026-04-06 15:29:20.976+00
222f4314-3643-413e-b1dc-77c19bdbba7d	New initiative	An I Love Jinshasan Tirth Yatra Initiative\n\nIt would be wonderful if our team could organise a special Tirth Yatra to a significant Jain pilgrimage destination, such as Palitana, Girnar or another Jain Tirth.\n\nThe yatra could be organised on a contribution or registration-fee basis, making it accessible to interested members of the Jain community, including youngsters, families and first-time participants.\n\nWhat could make it unique is having an I Love Jinshasan-led experience throughout the journey, including brief introductions to the historical and spiritual significance of each Tirth, group interactions, inspiring discussions and opportunities to connect with fellow participants.\n\nWe could also invite non-members to join, giving more people an opportunity to discover I Love Jinshasan and become part of its initiatives.\n\nThis could become an annual initiative that combines Tirth Darshan, learning, community bonding and spiritual growth, while helping our organisation reach and connect with more people.	\N	d22fd755-c0a9-4474-8280-64d42c126700	reviewed	\N	2026-11-02 07:59:08.630606+00	2026-11-05 07:48:49.104+00
922753ed-0f14-4c15-aa60-919d55699748	Difference between syadvad and anekantvad	I have generally observed between jain and non jain community that people are not clear with the difference between these two concepts. If some teacher or sir speaks about it ,the students are going to believe it irrespective of it being actually true or no..because sometimes teachers( especially of other communities) themselves dont the difference. It would be nice if it comes from sahebji's end so that we can explain and prove its authenticity.	\N	1faa2e14-2817-46d8-8f9e-5e7b70855a98	reviewed	Thank you for suggesting this idea. We have taken note of this.	2026-03-23 13:00:25.421875+00	2026-05-06 11:19:12.223+00
5373b451-2eaa-4e4d-a343-5f9a993fce00	UI/UX design suggestions for Spiritual Tasks and gamification of the same	Currently in the Spiritual Task, one can only mark the task as completed if they have done that consistently for 7 days minimum but at times we do a task but it may happen consistently for 7 days. I had talked to Aayushi ben about this and was assured that even if we complete the task on 7 non-consecutive days, we can mark as complete which is very helpful. I do have 3 suggestions for this regardless:\n\n1) Instead of just marking it as complete, we can let volunteers mark as checkbox and have 7 boxes/circles so they can tick/mark it as and when they do because in todays busy schedule we often forget if we completed  6 days or 7 days and it can create wrong markings as well. And making ticks boxes also encourages us to tick multiple items in a day.\nExample - One day I read a book and sat in silence while also wrote things and people I am grateful about, I can individually mark all 3 tasks and see a progress bar which encourages me to stay consistent.\nThis also helps to do multiple things in a day as once the task is marked as completed, our psyche forgets about it and we may be less likely to continue for more than 7 days.\n\n2) Have it for more than 7 days, sure we can mark the task as completed once we hit the 7 mark checkpoint but it can reset and continue for more, this gamifies the process and encourages to continue in life more. Prime example would be of Duolingo, the language learning app. You hit 7 days mark of learning, hurray, now aim for 10 days and so one. It can either reset or continue, the later is prefered.\n\n3) Have the option to unmark/undo as well, if someone is browsing for the first time or if someone accidently marks it as complete, there is no way to go back, this also results in inaccuracy, once 7 days are consistently marked, it's submitted but prior to that, marking whole thing as one big task, atleast we can have an undo button to correct our mistakes.	\N	f854a439-e72d-44fc-8efe-a0f755462a04	reviewed	\N	2026-05-21 08:34:19.477881+00	2026-06-02 18:51:39.943+00
a6426f44-9667-4a30-97b2-18b687eac777	Jainific - beyond learning	One idea I would love to suggest is making the event more experience-based rather than limiting it to information-based stalls. We could introduce a common interactive format where every stall has a quick 1–2 minute challenge, mystery activity, audience voting question or hands-on task related to its subject. Visitors could receive a small participation card and collect stamps after completing activities at different stalls.\n\nWe could also have a collective creation wall where every visitor contributes an idea, thought, drawing or small commitment, gradually building something meaningful throughout the event.\n\nThe best part is that these activities can be customised for every theme, require minimal materials and budget, and encourage visitors to actively participate rather than just walk around and read. The aim would be to make every visitor leave having experienced something, learnt something and contributed something.	134f3fc8-8546-4bb3-b39a-e671bfcb0808	d22fd755-c0a9-4474-8280-64d42c126700	reviewed	\N	2026-11-02 07:55:09.503835+00	2026-11-05 07:49:26.472+00
a179f147-d36d-41df-a4e5-e0d56b4f27bd	1 reflection Sunday - from content to change	While our current initiatives - reels, quizzes, and digital content...are highly engaging, there is an opportunity to further strengthen their impact by ensuring they lead to real-life behavioral change, not just consumption.\nI propose introducing a structured weekly initiative called “Reflection Sunday.”\nThe idea is to dedicate one day each week to pause, reflect, and assess how the content shared throughout the week has influenced individual actions.\nEvery Sunday, the community can be prompted with 2–3 simple yet meaningful questions such as:\nWhat is one value or practice you implemented this week?\nWhat motivated you to take that step?\nWhat challenges did you face while applying it?\nThis can be seamlessly integrated using existing platforms like WhatsApp or Instagram, or via a simple Google Form—requiring minimal additional resources.\nA few authentic responses can then be shared (with consent) to highlight real stories of change, thereby inspiring others within the community.\nThis initiative aligns strongly with the core philosophy of Jainism, particularly the practice of self-reflection and introspection (similar to Pratikraman), but reimagined in a format that resonates with today’s youth.\nProblem it addresses:\nCurrently, there is no structured mechanism to track whether our content is translating into meaningful personal growth or lifestyle changes.\nImpact:\nTransforms passive engagement into active implementation\nEncourages consistency in practicing values\nBuilds a culture of self-awareness and accountability\nStrengthens the emotional and spiritual depth of the community\nIn essence, this initiative can help evolve our platform from being content-driven to transformation-driven, creating a more lasting and meaningful impact.	deed7a1e-10f7-4f6a-906a-aac54bb1f619	d22fd755-c0a9-4474-8280-64d42c126700	reviewed	Can you please provide us a specific to-do list or a task Which Will help us to implement this idea	2026-03-24 02:40:07.398353+00	2026-04-06 15:25:35.545+00
